\begin{abstract} % TC:break abstract
Mountain glaciers are projected to lose between 26 and 41~\unit{\%} of
their mass from 2015 until 2100 depending on the assumed global
temperature change scenario.
Melting glacier ice contributes to sea-level rise and reduces terrestrial
freshwater storage.
Monitoring glacier change contributes to the evidence base that can help
affected communities assess whether adaptation is necessary and identify
appropriate measures.
We introduce the open-source Python package CryoSwath, which estimates
glacier surface elevations from freely available \cs{} altimetry data.
Alongside, we establish a process-informed interpolation method and
estimate uncertainty correlations from systematic comparison to a
photogrammetry-based reference (ArcticDEM strips).
Further, we present a CryoSwath-produced dataset of monthly sampled, three-month rolling-window
glacier surface elevation estimates at a 500~\unit{m} resolution
covering nearly all glaciated areas in the Arctic during the period 2010
to 2024.
We find that glaciers in Alaska are thinning most rapidly while those in Arctic Canada
North and Greenland Periphery show the slowest region-wide change rates.
However, negative trends are ubiquitous with an Arctic-wide mass loss
of \VAR{trends[("Arctic-wide", "___")]|r2v("mass")|fmt(absolute=True)}.

The dataset opens research opportunities to study these changes in detail
and to contextualize the glacier response to variability in atmospheric
or oceanic forcing.
The open software enables non-expert researchers from different fields to
efficiently process \cs{} data at scale over glaciated regions
and inspect the data at intermediate processing stages.
The user has full control over the processing and, with that, the means
to derive and output non-default quantities.
\end{abstract}


\introduction  %% \introduction[modified heading if necessary]
% TC:break intro

In our rapidly changing climate, together with other Earth system
components, the cryosphere constantly adapts. Mountain glaciers in
particular are a visible example of how global warming affects our
planet, as they react over timescales of a few years to decades to
increasing temperatures. The shrinking and retreat of these freshwater reservoirs
not only affect global sea level but also have profound effects on
local freshwater availability, ecosystems, and economic activities,
and may trigger hazards like flooding and landslides. Monitoring
glaciers to improve our understanding of their current and future
behavior is, thus, both of great scientific and societal interest.
\citet{rounce2023GlobalGlacierChange} project glacier mass losses of
26 to 41~\unit{\%} between 2015 and 2100 for global warming scenarios
of 1.5 to 4~\unit{\degree C} relative to preindustrial levels.

In-situ measurements of glacier mass balance provide invaluable
information on the state of mountain glaciers, yet can only cover a
small fraction of the approximately 270,000 glaciers on our planet.
Remote sensing provides a platform to map glaciers on a global scale and
has accelerated our understanding of how glaciers respond to changes in
atmospheric and oceanic forcing
\citep{gardner2013ReconciledEstimateGlacier,
wouters2019GlobalGlacierMass, ciraci2020ContinuityMassLoss,
berthier2023MeasuringGlacierMass, dussaillant2025AnnualMassChange,
theglambieteam2025CommunityEstimateGlobal}.

Satellite gravimetry by the GRACE and \mbox{GRACE-FO} missions measures mass
changes directly at monthly resolution. However, the horizontal
resolution is on the order of 100 kilometers, too coarse to resolve
individual glaciers and separate smaller mountain glaciers from other
sources of mass variations in their surroundings, e.g., due to
hydrological processes.

An indirect way of assessing glacier mass balance is by considering their change in elevation.
Digital elevation models (DEMs) of glaciers based on either optical
stereo-photogrammetry or bistatic InSAR obtained at different moments in
time can be differenced to obtain elevation change estimates. The
resulting datasets are typically of high spatial resolution
[$\mathcal{O}$(1--10~\unit{m})] but lower temporal resolution. The latter is,
partly, because of a lack of dedicated missions for this purpose, but
also due to, e.g., the dependence on clear-sky and daylight conditions
for optical imagery, and penetration of the radar signal into the snow
and firn layer for InSAR, which requires that, ideally, DEMs from
approximately the same time of the year are differenced to reduce biases
related to changes in the penetration depth (see
\citet{berthier2023MeasuringGlacierMass} for a detailed discussion). The
elevation changes obtained through geodetic methods like DEM-differencing require assumptions
about the density of the observed volume changes to be translated to
mass balance estimates, a non-trivial step that introduces major
uncertainties \citep{huss2013DensityAssumptionsConverting}.

Another way of estimating glacier elevation changes is through repeat
satellite altimetry. ICESat, the first laser altimetry mission dedicated
to cryosphere applications, was launched in 2003, followed by \mbox{ICESat-2}
in 2018. Altimetry does not depend on daylight conditions, but when
using laser, it still depends on clear-sky conditions. A main advantage
of laser altimetry is the high precision and accuracy of surface
elevation measurements for a given, small footprint, on the order of
10~\unit{m} in the case of \mbox{ICESat-2}. The small footprint,
however, leads to a
sparser spatiotemporal coverage compared to radar altimetry. 

While the footprint and accuracy of early radar missions was
insufficient to study mountain glaciers, this changed with the launch of
the first SARIn (see below) altimeter, \cs, the European Space Agency's (ESA) mission dedicated to measuring
surface elevation changes in the cryosphere
\citep{wingham2006CryoSatMissionDetermine}. The original mission design
targeted sea ice and large ice sheets
\citep{drinkwater2004EuropeanSpaceAgencys}. This mission, for the first
time, also measured the angle of arrival of the echo in the so-called
SARIn mode, using a second antenna that is mounted perpendicular to the
direction of flight, allowing to precisely geolocate the echo point in
sloping terrain. 

Traditionally, radar altimetry retracks the recorded signal echo to
derive the range to the point of closest approach (POCA), using the
first increase and peak of the power of the echo waveform.
\citet{hawley2009IcesheetElevationsAcrosstrack} suggested exploiting the full
recording of the return signal may increase the number of across-track
elevation measurements by an order of magnitude.
\citet{gray2013InterferometricSwathProcessing} proved the
concept---which became known as ``swath processing''---and its
applicability to \cs{} data and mountain glaciers. Today, there is a
large body of studies that rely on \cs{} swath elevation estimates
\citep{foresta2016SurfaceElevationChange, foresta2018HeterogeneousRapidIce,
jakob2021SpatiallyTemporallyResolved, jakob2023GlacierMassLoss}. ESA
provides public point and gridded elevation products through CryoTEMPO-EOLIS,
including uncertainty estimates. The Baseline 3 point-product variables
listed in its handbook (Issue 4.1) do not include waveform power or coherence
\citep{cryotempo-eolis2026CryoTEMPOEOLISProductHandbook}. Access to these
products does not provide control over the processing choices used to
derive them. Furthermore, the standard workflow applied to the
\cs{} waveforms to derive surface elevations may not always align with
the needs of the end user, when they want to perform advanced analyses.

Here, we introduce CryoSwath, a free and open-source Python package that
processes ESA's freely available \cs{} SARIn L1b waveform data and
produces glacier surface elevation estimates. Designed to enable
non-expert radar altimetry data users to swath process \cs{} data,
CryoSwath provides both pipelines using predefined default parameters
and full control over processing the data at all stages. Alongside,
we publish monthly sampled, three-month rolling-window surface elevation data in a 500~\unit{m} square grid
for the mountain glaciers and ice caps of the Arctic derived by
CryoSwath 0.2.2 and time series of average surface elevation changes.

