\section{Initial data}

In this section, we introduce the datasets that we build on. These are
ESA's \cs{} SARIn L1b product at baseline E
\citep{europeanspaceagency2019L1bSARinPrecise}, the digital elevation
model (DEM) ArcticDEM at version 4.1
\citep{porter2023ArcticDEMMosaicsVersion}, and glacier outlines and
other data of the Randolph Glacier Inventory (RGI) at version 7.0
\citep{rgi7}.

\subsection{ESA's SARIn L1b product}

As a Ku-band radar altimeter, \cs{} transmits a radar pulse and receives
part of the signal that is reflected by matter at or below the
surface, termed ``scatterers''. For glacier settings, this means that
the back-scattered signal will typically be a combination of reflections
from the surface, and reflections from the snow volume, caused by
density variations within the snowpack. The interested reader may refer
to
\citet{morris2022CryoSat2InterferometricMode} to learn about the
relation between seasons and the scatterer location relative to the
surface.
Based on the travel time, the distance to the echo location can be estimated.
\cs{} operates in three different modes: a low-resolution mode, a
Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) mode, and a SAR Interferometric (SARIn)
mode.
SAR uses multiple coherently transmitted signals to synthesize an
effectively larger antenna along the satellite's flight path
\citep{raney1998DelayDopplerRadar}.
The interferometric measurements require a second antenna, mounted
perpendicular to the travel direction.
Comparing the recordings of both yields a phase difference that allows us
to determine the across-track direction to the origin of the reflected
signal. SARIn mode is active over glaciers and, further, is a
prerequisite for swath processing since only SARIn delivers the critical
phase difference information. SARIn acquisition is governed by ESA's
geographic mode mask, so glacier coverage is limited to areas observed
in this mode \citep{europeanspaceagency2023NewCryoSatGeographical}.

In SARIn mode, \cs{} sends and receives about 21 coherent signal bursts per
second.
Each burst consists of 64 pulses at a pulse repetition frequency of about
18~\unit{kHz}.
Using delay/Doppler processing \citep{raney1998DelayDopplerRadar}, the
echoes of each burst are separated along-track into 64 Doppler beams,
illuminating narrow footprints of roughly 400~\unit{m} along-track
and 15~\unit{km} across-track (half-power beam width).
In the range direction, the measurement window spans about 240~\unit{m}.
As \cs{} moves along its orbit, each ground location is observed by
Doppler beams from successive bursts, typically yielding about 50 -- 60
individual looks in SARIn mode.
These are aligned and averaged to reduce noise (``multilooking'').
The L1b product provides about 20 multilooked ``waveforms'' per second,
each containing the aggregated information for one ground location.
The L1b data include power, coherence, and phase difference recordings
in 1024 samples over the range window, potentially each bearing
information about the surface at some location.
Note that the data are oversampled by a factor of 2 and that neighboring
samples are related to each other.
If the recorded power is much larger than the noise, we attempt to
recover the reflection's origin.

ESA also distributes processed elevations through
\href{https://cryotempo-eolis.org/}{CryoTEMPO-EOLIS}
\citep{gourmelen2018CryoSat2SwathInterferometric,
jakob2023GlacierMassLoss, cryotempo-eolis2026CryoTEMPOEOLISProductHandbook}.
CryoSwath derives its elevations directly from the L1b waveforms.

\subsection{Reference topography}

We employ the digital elevation model (DEM) ArcticDEM 100~\unit{m}
mosaics at version 4.1 \citep{porter2023ArcticDEMMosaicsVersion} to
aid in locating the echo origins of \cs's signal.
ArcticDEM is based on photogrammetry.
We also use ArcticDEM to mitigate potential biases from changes in the
data distribution by considering the differences between
\cs-retrieved elevation estimates and ArcticDEM.


\subsection{Glacier and drainage basin extents}

We use the glacier basin extents given by the Randolph Glacier Inventory
(RGI) version 7.0 \citep{rgi7} to identify glacierized areas.
Further, we use RGI data to summarize the presented data for first- and
second-order regions as defined by the RGI.
First-order regions, e.g., Alaska, can contain multiple second-order
regions, e.g., North Alaska, Alaska Range, and more.
We use the term region for first-order regions, while we refer to
second-order regions as subregions.
The studied regions are Alaska, Arctic Canada North and
South, Greenland Periphery, Iceland, Svalbard and Jan Mayen,
Scandinavia, and Russian Arctic.
One exception, where we do not follow the RGI second-order convention,
is the Greenland periphery.
While in the RGI dataset, it constitutes a single subregion, we divide it
into 5 subregions: North Greenland, West Greenland, Southwest Greenland,
Southeast Greenland, and East Greenland.
The lists of the RGI glacier and complex identifiers for each
custom subregion are available (Sect.~\ref{sec:dataAvail}).


\section{Algorithm}

With the data described above, we are equipped to derive \cs{} swath
point surface elevation estimates and higher-order products, e.g.,
rasterized surface elevation change rates. We explain the algorithm
in this section, starting from the input data, describing echo
geolocation, resolving the phase ambiguity, rasterizing point elevation
estimates, fitting a trend model, interpolating voids, and computing
regional mass change time series. Echo geolocation and phase ambiguity
resolution build on established swath processing methods
\citep{gourmelen2018CryoSat2SwathInterferometric,
garcia-mondejar2019MultisurfaceRetrackerSwath}, with CryoSwath's
modifications described below.
The flowchart in Appendix Fig.~\ref{fig:flowchart} provides an overview of the workflow.
Note that the most recent release of its Python
implementation can be found on GitHub at
\href{https://github.com/j-haacker/cryoswath}{\texttt{\small
github.com/j-haacker/cryoswath}}.
CryoSwath can be installed using the package managers
\href{https://pypi.org/project/cryoswath/}{pip} or
\href{https://anaconda.org/conda-forge/cryoswath}{conda}, or can be used
inside a \href{https://hub.docker.com/r/cryoswath/jupyterlab}{Docker container}.
The version used for the present data is 0.2.2.


\subsection{Data selection}
\label{sec:algo:data_selection}

With the L1b data product, ESA provides indicators (flags) that help to recognize
potentially bad data.
We discard waveforms that are flagged with one or more of the
``measurement confidence data'' flags (\verb|flag_mcd_20_ku|)
stated in Table~\ref{tab:mcd_flags}
\citep{scagliola2020CryoSatIceNetCDF} to
reduce the risk of polluting the dataset with erroneous measurements.
While this is CryoSwath's default choice, it allows any user-defined selection.
These are ESA's input flags; the coherence, power, and swath-start
criteria below are CryoSwath's configurable filtering choices.
To reduce computational costs, we exclude waveforms where the
satellite's ground position is 30~\unit{km} or further away from any
glacier because the antenna gain is
too small to receive any signal \citep[$<\,-$40~\unit{dB},
see][]{gray2013InterferometricSwathProcessing}.
Further, we ignore waveform samples for which the coherence is lower than
0.6.
The coherence is close to 1, if a single, small area reflects much more
of the signal than other sources. If, on the other hand, there are
multiple areas reflecting similarly strong signals, the coherence is
small with a minimum value of 0.
Again, CryoSwath allows the user to choose the coherence threshold.
CryoSwath further defaults to excluding samples before and just after
the first peak of the coherence.
The definition of the excluded part can be summarized as all samples
before the coherence rises again after its first peak.
This is an important step that separates the usable data from data that
are especially prone to systematic errors
\citep{haacker2023SystematicErrorsObserved}.

In the following, we express the range distance between waveform samples
in meters because these distances also have physical meanings that
relate to glacier surface properties, e.g., roughness. The distance
between two adjacent waveform samples is 0.2342~\unit{m}. The stated
distances should be translated to waveform sample numbers by rounding down to
the next lower integer. For example, if waveform sample number $b$ is 5~\unit{m} behind waveform sample number $a$ in range direction, then $b = a +
\mathrm{floor(5/0.2342)} = a + 21$.

CryoSwath uses a sophisticated algorithm that balances harvesting data
and the risk of polluting the dataset by identifying the initial
waveform part that is associated with returns from the POCA.
Samples before that corresponding to the POCA should not be swath
processed, since the POCA is the surface at the smallest range.
Over oceans and flat parts of ice sheets, the location of the POCA-sample relative
to the extent of the leading edge of the power waveform can be chosen
such that the sample corresponds to an estimate of the elevation of the
air-snow, -firn, -ice, or -water interface.
Over mountain glaciers, this strategy is not easily applicable because
the complex topography translates to a complex power waveform.
Luckily, the coherence is a robust measure of the spatial scatterer density at the corresponding range
that can be used alternatively. However, there are subtle differences in the
interpretation: the first coherence peak, corresponding to the first
large scatterer density, is not necessarily at the surface, but can be
at a dense layer within the snowpack or firn (see section ``scattering
horizon''
below). In the context of this layer, the first coherence peak marks the
closest distance to the satellite and will here, in the context of swath
processing, be treated like the conventional POCA.
CryoSwath finds the first waveform sample that passes the coherence
threshold (0.6 here).
It identifies the POCA at the maximum
coherence within 10~\unit{m} (default value) after the
first sample that exceeded the coherence threshold.
After the POCA, the coherence first decreases before it starts
to increase again \citep{gray2013InterferometricSwathProcessing}.
The algorithm detects increases in the low-pass filtered coherence that
exceed 0.001 per sample in the range between 5 and 55~\unit{m} (default
value) after the POCA sample.
The detected sample is the first to be swath-processed, later referred
to as ``swath start''.
\begin{figure}[t]
    \includegraphics[width=8.3cm]{\VAR{"fig02__example__wf_start.pdf"|fig}}
    \caption{
        Exemplary coherence waveforms.
        The blue dots and the orange crosses show the coherence of each
        sample of the waveform.
        The blue-dot-samples will lead to point elevation estimates
        later, while the orange-cross samples will be discarded.
        The gray line shows the low-pass filtered coherence.
        The filled green circle indicates the coherence at the point of
        closest approach (POCA).
        Panel (a) shows an example of the regular case and panel (b)
        shows an example for a class of special cases where \cs{}
        receives a signal starting from the first sample.
    }
    \label{fig:ex_wf_start}
\end{figure}
The mentioned waveform sections and samples are shown at hand of an
example in Fig.~\ref{fig:ex_wf_start}a.
Should no coherence increase be detected in the given range, the
waveform is disregarded entirely.

If \cs{} has misaligned the range window, it is possible that the window
does not start before the first surfaces are illuminated.
Such cases are recognized if the coherence threshold is exceeded in the
first 10~\unit{m} of the range window.
Then, CryoSwath swath processes all
samples, including those at the beginning of the waveform.
An example of the case is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:ex_wf_start}b.

Finally, we discard weak signals, i.e., waveform samples with a
signal-to-noise ratio less than 10 (default value). The L1b product
includes a noise power estimate for each waveform. However, the noise
value is volatile and sometimes unrealistically high. In such cases, the
signal-to-noise threshold can lead to disregarding waveform samples that
do not appear to be noisy when inspected. By default, and as used for the
present data, CryoSwath does not use the provided noise values but
calculates the noise
from the received signal power at the beginning of waveforms over the
entire track.
In detail, for each waveform, we calculate the average received power of the first 30
samples.
We then calculate the minimum values for rolling windows of 100
waveforms, corresponding to about 30~\unit{km} along-track, in forward
and backward directions.
For each waveform, the larger of the two minimum values defines the
noise power estimate.


\subsection{Apparent echo origin location}
\label{sec:algo:geolocation}

\begin{figure}[t]
    \includegraphics[width=8.3cm]{\VAR{"fig03__geometry_sketch.png"|fig}}
    \caption{
        Two cross-sections, panel (a) along and panel (b) across \cs's
        track, that visualize $P_\text{CS2}$, $P_\text{scat}$, h, r,
        $\theta$, and $\Delta \zeta$ that are used in the location of the
        echo origin. Additionally, the reference ellipsoid WGS84, the polar
        axis, nadir (on the reference ellipsoid), the origin of the
        reference frame, and the topography are indicated. Note that the
        measures are not to scale but are chosen such that meaningful
        distances are large enough to be recognized.}
    \label{fig:sketch}
\end{figure}

Please refer to the sketch in Fig.~\ref{fig:sketch} for visual guidance.
Note that we can only retrieve the apparent, i.e., the calculated echo
origin, which can differ from the actual one for a number of reasons
(see Sect.~\ref{sec:errors}).
Further, note that the reference frame for the calculations is shifted
such that its origin coincides with the intersection of the WGS84-normal
at the satellite's coordinates and the polar axis. Vectors that describe
points relative to the origin of this reference frame will be named
$\vec{P_\text{xxx}}$, with xxx being some descriptive subscript.

We calculate the location of the echo origin from the position of the
satellite $\vec{P_\text{CS2}}$, the satellite's travel direction
$\alpha_\text{CS2}$, the satellite-scatterer distance
$\overline{P_\text{CS2}P_\text{scat}}$, and the nadir-scatterer angle
$\theta$, also known as angle of arrival.
The satellite position $\vec{P_\text{CS2}}$ is given by the initial data as latitude $\phi$,
longitude $\lambda$, and height above the reference ellipsoid.
\cs's travel direction $\alpha_\text{CS2}$ is estimated: we calculate the azimuths between all
satellite positions of the current track extent; we then use a
least-squares regression to fit a third-order polynomial to the
azimuths; finally, we evaluate the polynomial at the location
corresponding to the satellite's position.
The satellite-scatterer distance $\overline{P_\text{CS2}P_\text{scat}}$ is calculated from the delay
between transmitting and receiving the signal, the speed of light in
vacuum, and the waveform sample number.
We correct the distance $\overline{P_\text{CS2}P_\text{scat}}$ for the
effects of the ionosphere, the dry and wet troposphere, and the pole,
solid-earth, and load tides.
Those corrections are provided with the L1b product.
The angle $\theta$ is calculated from the roll angle $\chi$, the distance between the two
antenna centers $b$, the signal frequency $f_\text{Ku}$, and the phase difference $\Delta\beta$:
$\theta = \arcsin\left(
    \frac{\Delta\beta \cdot c}{f_\text{Ku} \cdot 2\pi\cdot b}
    \right) - \chi$.
The angle $\theta$ is on the order of 1~\unit{\degree}.

The coordinate derivation is given in Appendix~\ref{app:geolocation}.

For the current dataset, actually, we low-pass filter the phase difference
before calculating the echo origins as described above.
We do so to mitigate the impact of noise on the later surface elevation
aggregates of areas with low data density.
The default used here is a Gaussian-weighted average over a
rolling window of 21 samples with a standard deviation of 5 samples.
The chosen low-pass filter balances reducing fluctuations that increase
when the received power decreases and maintaining a large number of
independent elevation estimates by limiting the filter scope.
We explored different filter settings, including no low-pass filtering,
and found the results to be robust.


\subsection{Phase ambiguity}

We will use the phase
difference recording to retrieve the echo location.
Depending on the nadir-scatterer angle $\theta$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:sketch}), the
echo travels different distances to each of the two antennas. This path
length difference maps to a difference in the phase of the signal
received at the antennas, measured by \cs's sensor.
However, we cannot tell the absolute phase difference since it wraps at
$2\pi$. This introduces an ambiguity: the phase difference could be
$n\cdot2\pi$ ``off'', with $n\in\mathbb{Z}$. In consequence, there are a
number of theoretically possible nadir-scatterer angles. To resolve this
ambiguity, we compare the resulting possible scatterer locations to a
DEM.
We calculate possible locations for $n\in[-3..3]$ as described in
Appendix~\ref{app:geolocation} and
deem the locations that are closer to the DEM more probable than those
further away.
In this section, we describe the implemented algorithm to
choose $n$.

The algorithm is an extension of the work of
\citet{garcia-mondejar2019MultisurfaceRetrackerSwath}, previously
published in \citet{haacker2023SystematicErrorsObserved}. Basically,
surfaces are assumed to be continuous and consecutive waveform samples
are expected to differ only slightly in the phase difference.
In practice, this assumption can be violated when the footprint
simultaneously illuminates distinct surfaces (e.g.\ opposing valley
slopes) and the dominant scattering contribution switches between them.
The algorithm therefore splits the waveform where such switching is
likely. This creates groups of samples, in which continuity is assumed.
Two independent exceptions define where the waveform is split into
groups. The first one typically occurs when two distinct surfaces
separated by more than 5~\unit{km} are illuminated together, and the
second one on shorter distances.

The first exception is satisfied if (i) three or more consecutive samples
either are excluded from swath processing because they do not meet the
global coherence and power criteria, here 0.6 and ten times the noise
power, respectively, or (ii) the sample's original phase difference,
independent of the optional
low-pass filtering (see above), deviates more than 0.1~\unit{rad} from
the smoothed reference. The power and phase difference thresholds
prevent two types of signals from unintentionally connecting sample
groups: signals that exceed the coherence threshold but are very weak,
and signals that originate from a different location.

The second exception is satisfied if the smoothed reference changes
faster than 0.1~\unit{rad} between neighboring samples, or
0.16~\unit{rad} from sample number $i$ to $i+2$ (two times 80~\unit{\%}
of 0.1~\unit{rad}). CryoSwath allows modifying all thresholds, but any
custom settings should be tested for the expected behavior because of
their interdependency. Note that CryoSwath features diagnostic
visualizations to inspect the waveforms, e.g.,
\lstinline|cryoswath.test_plots.waveform.dem_transect()|.

The approach described above results in groups of waveform samples, resulting from
cutting the waveform where the two mentioned exceptions occur.
For each group and possible values for $n$, CryoSwath considers two
measures: the median difference to the reference DEM, and the median
absolute deviation \citep{hohle2009AccuracyAssessmentDigital} from the
reference DEM.
The first evaluates the difference from the expected surface elevation,
and the second, whether the samples resemble the shape of the reference.
Subsequently, $n$ is chosen such that the sum of the squares of both is
minimized. Therefore, those solutions that are far from the reference,
or those that disagree on the shape, are penalized.
CryoSwath accepts custom functions for selecting the phase-wrap factor.


\subsection{Rasterization of point data} \label{sec:algo:rasterization}

After resolving the phase ambiguity, we arrive at point elevation
estimates. This stage is often referred to as level 2 (L2) data. Each
L1b waveform results in up to 1024 point elevation estimates; however,
usually many (order 100) waveform samples are discarded by one of the
filters described above (before swath start, low coherence, or low
signal-to-noise ratio).
Note that CryoSwath users can pass any variable from the L1b data to the
L2 data. This can be used to inspect, e.g., the coherence or power that
are used as quality indicators to filter the data (see above). In
practice, the user would pass
\lstinline|retain_vars=["coherence_waveform_20_ku", "power_waveform_20_ku"]|
to any function that returns L2 data, e.g.,
\lstinline|cryoswath.l2.process_and_save()|.

For the remaining computation, we subtract the surface elevations of a
reference DEM, here, ArcticDEM, from our derived point elevation
estimates, resulting in the difference $h_\Delta$. We consider these
differences to mitigate a potential bias from an uneven distribution of
data points over the surface.
Absolute differences exceeding 150~\unit{m} are discarded, i.e., all
remaining satisfy $|h_\Delta| \le 150$~\unit{m}.
Even when we do not explicitly say so, any
reference to surface elevations refers to $h_\Delta$.

We aggregate the L2 swath elevation data
within 500 by 500~\unit{m} grid cells and within a rolling three-month
window, which we found to best balance resolution and uncertainty,
resulting
in level 3 (L3) data.
First, we calculate the median of the elevation differences $h_\Delta$.
Further, to estimate the uncertainty of the median difference, we calculate
the interquartile range of the differences and count the number of data
points in each grid cell.
While these are the default choices in CryoSwath, the spatial
resolution, the temporal resolution, the aggregation period, the
calculated statistics, and whether and which outliers should be excluded
can be freely chosen by the user.


\subsection{Computing elevation trends} \label{sec:algo:elev_trend}

For the rasterized elevation estimates, we derive the linear trend by
least-squares fitting a model to the data. Note that we do not attempt to
offer generic solutions for processing beyond L3 data. However,
CryoSwath's modularity allows replacing or extending the implemented model.
The model selected here contains, in addition to the trend parameter, a
yearly and semi-yearly cycle.
Its mathematical formulation
follows in Eq.~\ref{eq:trend_with_season}.
\begin{equation} \label{eq:trend_with_season}
    h_\Delta(t) = c + m \cdot t 
    + A_{\textrm{full}} \cos(2\pi \cdot t - \phi_{\textrm{full}})
    + A_{\textrm{semi}} \cos(4\pi \cdot t - \phi_{\textrm{semi}})
\end{equation}
In Eq.~\ref{eq:trend_with_season}, the independent variable $t$ is the time
in years.
Further, $c$ denotes a constant
offset, $m$ is the surface elevation change trend, $A_{\textrm{full}}$ and $A_{\textrm{semi}}$ are
the cycle amplitudes of the yearly and semi-yearly components,
respectively, the indexed $\phi$ are the cycle phases with indices as before.

Grid cell time step aggregates that are based on less than 4 point
elevation estimates or those, where the interquartile range is larger
than 30~\unit{m}, are excluded from further evaluation.
We require that at least 5 of the 30 first and 5 of the 30 last time steps
contain elevation estimates to exclude poorly sampled grid cells.
We found the chosen values
to form a consensus for all studied regions, reducing the prevalence of
poorly sampled time series while maintaining sufficient spatial
coverage. Systematic optimization is not in the scope of the current
study and would probably require an individual analysis of the regions.
Those time steps for which the residual is larger than two standard
deviations of the residuals of the grid cell in question are considered
outliers and the model is fitted again, disregarding the outliers.

Subsequently, we exclude grid cells of which the trend parameter
variance is larger than 2~\unit{m^2}~\unit{yr^{-2}} or either of the
amplitude variances exceeds 100~\unit{m^2}.
The exclusion thresholds have been chosen empirically, striving to
balance excluding seemingly implausible data while limiting
false positives. This exclusion criterion, as well as the
outlier-removal above, are not expected to bias trend estimates, but
could, however, artificially lower the trend uncertainty estimate.
Quantifying the choices' impacts is not in the scope of the current
study. Note that it would require reference data in a variety of regions
and during different seasons.


\subsection{Void interpolation} \label{sec:algo:voids}

There are voids in the spatial trend data and in the rasterized monthly
elevation estimates. We fill voids for both kinds of data using almost
the same method. We explain our approach below in detail, structured in
processing ``Steps''. The data products include flags that indicate how
each cell was filled, with directly observed data being indicated by a
flag value of zero. The flag values mostly match the following Step
numbers, however, with exceptions in Step~2 and~3. These output flags
are distinct from ESA's input measurement-confidence flags used for data
selection (Sect.~\ref{sec:algo:data_selection}).

\textbf{Step 1:} In a first step, for those
grid cells where a model could be fitted (see previous section) all
missing time step values in the monthly
dataset are filled by
evaluating the model.
The uncertainty of the filled grid cells is estimated by two times the
root-mean-square error of the fitted models.
Concerned cells are flagged with the value one.

\textbf{Step 2:} Afterward, in both datasets, we attempt to mask
areas that were ice-free most of the time.
These areas would otherwise bias the later void-filling because they
show no elevation change with a low uncertainty while not representing
glacierized areas.
The RGI glacier outlines contain ice-free areas because they represent
the glaciers as they were around the year 2000, while the present dataset starts only in
2010.
To determine which areas are mostly ice-free, we analyze average grid
cell trends per elevation band for each basin.
Elevation bands are chosen as required for the hypsometric
interpolation in Step~3.2. In detail, they are chosen depending on the
glacier's 10th to 90th percentile elevation span (10-90-span) according
to the reference DEM. If the 10-90-span is less than 500~\unit{m}, the
bands are chosen such that they span one tenth of the 10-90-span. Else, the
bands span 50~\unit{m}. The lowest band is aligned with the lowest
elevation of the glacier surface.
For ice-free elevation bands, the average elevation trend is close to
zero.
If the lowest band is ice-free, the lowest glacierized band shows the
largest absolute elevation trend.
We search and find the maximum absolute average trend or elevation
difference of the lowest five elevation bands for the trend dataset or
the monthly dataset, respectively. The lowest band of which the absolute
average trend or elevation difference is larger than half
of the maximum is considered as the front of the glacier. Lower grid
cells are excluded from further evaluation.
The above assumes that the glacier either retreats or advances.
If that is not the case, the average elevation trends of the five lowest
bands are similar which leads to not excluding any data.

\textbf{Step 3:} The following Steps~3.1--3.5 are executed consecutively
for individual basins (flag value two) and, then, for groups of basins
(flag value 3) that are chosen along termination type, latitude, and
longitude. More specifically, the termination types are read from RGI
and are either ``marine-terminating'' or ``unclassified''. Further, the
groups span about 1~\unit{\degree} latitude and 1~\unit{\degree} longitude divided by the cosine of latitude (to
maintain the same scale
across different latitudes). Repeating the Steps~3.1--3.5 for groups of
basins is necessary if there are insufficient data for individual
basins. In fact, there can be cases in which even for the basin groups
the available elevation estimates are insufficient for a number of time
steps or even for all. These cases are handled in Step~4, for the
monthly dataset, and Step~5, for both datasets.

\textbf{Step 3.1:} We attempt to filter noise by comparing
grid cells to their neighbors using the Z-score, i.e., a statistical
measure of deviation.
The Z-score is defined as the difference of a sample member to the
sample mean, scaled by the sample standard deviation.
We compare the Z-scores per grid cell of each dataset (trend data and
monthly data) with the DEM-based elevation Z-scores.
In detail, we calculate the Z-score in a 5 by 5 grid cell window, if
this window contains 6 or more
valid values.
If the difference between the DEM Z-scores and each of the datasets is
larger than two, we consider the grid cell in question a potential outlier
and discard its value.
By relating the dataset Z-score to the DEM Z-score, we dynamically adapt
the noise threshold to the topographic complexity.

\textbf{Step 3.2:} We continue by aggregating data in preparation for applying the
local mean hypsometric interpolation method
\citep{mcnabb2019SensitivityGlacierVolume}.
The local mean hypsometric interpolation method fits a third-order
polynomial to the average glacier elevation trends or differences of
elevation bands
and interpolates voids using the fitted model evaluated at the void's
surface elevation.
The surface elevations at the location of voids and of observations are
read from a reference DEM.
However, in many cases, the uncertainties of elevation trends or
differences spread across more than one order of magnitude.
We use a weighted average instead of the regular average, to mitigate
the influence of values with a large uncertainty on the average.
Here, the weights are the reciprocal trend-parameter variances
from the fits described in Sect.~\ref{sec:algo:elev_trend} for the trend
data, and the reciprocal interquartile range of point elevation
estimates for the monthly data.
Values that deviate more than two standard deviations from the weighted
average are discarded as outliers.
The effective number of observations that constitute the weighted
average is defined by $\left(\sum_i w_i\right)^2/\sum_i w_i^2$, with $w_i$
denoting the weight of cell $i$.
Thus, the effective number of observations is always lower than the
actual number of observations, and it is largest for roughly equal
weights.
In the subsequent fit, we consider only those bands for which the
effective number of observations is 6 or larger.

\textbf{Step 3.3:} For fitting the hypsometric model, we
require weighted averages for at least five elevation bands.
We use the ridge regression implementation of \texttt{scikit\allowbreak-learn}
\citep{pedregosa2011ScikitlearnMachineLearning,
scikit-learndevelopers2026Scikitlearn} with the singular value
decomposition solver that allows weighting the observations, to fit the
polynomial.
Next to weighting the residuals which will be explained below, this
approach is different from a regular least-squares regression because,
in addition to the square-error, the sum of squared parameter values is
minimized.
A test using the regular least-squares method led to large values for the
lowest and highest elevation bands for single basins, which seemed
unrealistic because neighboring glaciers did not show similar behavior
and no surge events, i.e., rapid acceleration of glacier flow speeds
that can lead to fast changes of the surface elevation, were reported
for the concerned glaciers.
We choose the mentioned ridge regression to penalize models that deviate
much from the equilibrium state, i.e., no elevation change across all
bands.
We weight residuals by the reciprocals of the weighted variance for each
elevation band divided by the effective number of observations.
In case of the monthly data, we impose a threshold on the maximum
absolute gradient of the third-order polynomial.
This is necessary to avoid unrealistic polynomials in extreme cases,
e.g., where a little weight is given to outer bands and a concentrated
set of highly weighted bands forces large parameters for the second and
third orders.
We require the absolute gradient to remain lower than 10~\unit{m} per
100~\unit{m} of elevation.
The hypsometric model is only fitted if there are data for 5 or more bands.
If fewer or no data are available, we attempt to fill the concerned cells in
the next larger scope.

\textbf{Step 3.4:} For elevations lower than the
first elevation band for which we could derive a weighted average and
for elevations higher than the highest valid band, we need to
extrapolate the model.
We extrapolate linearly, where the elevation lies no further outside the
value-covered range than one third of this range, and where the linear
extrapolation does not exceed the range of weighted averages including
their uncertainty. Where one of the two conditions is violated, we
extrapolate with the same value as the nearest valid band.

\textbf{Step 3.5:} Where the resulting hypsometric model
with its linear or constant-value extrapolation differs from the earlier
calculated 5 by 5 cell averages by more than two standard deviations, we
fill voids with the local average.
Otherwise, voids are filled by evaluating the hypsometric model.
The uncertainty of the filled grid cells is estimated by two times the
root-mean-square error of the fitted model.

\textbf{Step 4:} For the monthly dataset, we
attempt to fill remaining voids temporally:
Where the above method failed only for a subset of time steps, the
successfully filled time steps are linearly interpolated to fill
the missing time steps (flag value 4).
We do not interpolate where the void periods are longer than one year.

\textbf{Step 5:} We proceed by applying Steps~3.1 to~3.5 to entire subregions for both
datasets. The only difference is that elevation trends or differences
that would be considered outliers
following the above method, are not replaced but only excluded from
the statistics.
This acknowledges that cells of different basins may behave very
differently depending on the basin's state, e.g., surging, recovering
from a surge, or none of the previous two.
The uncertainty of the filled grid cells is estimated by two times the
root-mean-square error of the fitted models.
Concerned cells are indicated by a flag value of five.

\textbf{Step 6:} If voids remain in the monthly dataset after the steps above, they are filled
with the temporally closest value (flag value six).
Then, we set the uncertainty to 74~\unit{m} for the filled values.
This seemingly specific number has no physical meaning but merely
originates from converting a hypothetical interquartile range of 50~\unit{m}---again without physical meaning but well higher than that of
direct observations---to the equivalent standard deviation times two.
Filling voids with the temporally closest value especially concerns
June to October 2010 where \cs{} was not yet fully operational and fewer
tracks are available. The current Step~6 leaves no voids.


\subsection{Elevation and mass change trends} \label{sec:algo:regional_trend}

We calculate regional and Arctic-wide time series by spatially averaging
the monthly surface elevation estimates.
First, we decimate the rolling-window time series by selecting every
third month to remove elevation estimates that are directly related to
others. Specifically, the selected months are November, February, May,
and August.
Next, we fit the model described in Eq.~\ref{eq:trend_with_season}.
In Fig.~\ref{fig:ts:arctic} (Sect.~\ref{sec:results}), we show the monthly
data, the decimated data, the data with the seasonal signal removed,
and the fitted trend for the Arctic-wide glacier surface elevation
change.
We test the significance of the trend using a Student's t-test. For our
2010--2024 time series of 58 3-monthly intervals, the 52 degrees of
freedom remain, accounting for 6 fitted parameters.
For 52 degrees of freedom, the t-test rejects the null hypothesis of no
significant trend for t values exceeding $\pm2.006$ at
$\alpha=0.05$ (two-sided).
We convert the elevation changes to mass changes assuming the average
density of glacier volume changes $\rho = 850 \pm
60$~\unit{kg}~\unit{m^{-3}}
\citep{huss2013DensityAssumptionsConverting}.


\section{Errors and uncertainties}
\label{sec:errors}

\subsection{Location errors} \label{sec:errors:location}

Errors in the location of echo origins are the dominant source of
uncertainty for \cs{} swath elevation estimates.
Here, we briefly discuss three contributors: (a) $n$ was chosen
incorrectly in the phase unwrapping, (b) phase difference uncertainty, and (c) range-ambiguity
(superposition) errors.

\textbf{(a)} Wrong phase-wrap factors affect very few data points
because it requires better agreement with the reference DEM at one of
the ambiguous locations.
If there were multiple surfaces at similar ranges
in the footprint, the signal coherence is low (except one of the
surfaces reflects the signal substantially better) and the concerned
waveform samples are discarded by the coherence filter. Groups are even
less likely to be affected because the majority of group members would
need to fit the ambiguous location better, implying the ambiguous
location not only accidentally fits with regard to the average
elevation, but also fits with regard to the topography.

\textbf{(b)} Noise in the phase difference results in a location
error in the across-track direction,
typically between 50 and 150~\unit{m} before low-pass filtering.
These numbers are based on an analysis of samples with coherence larger than
0.95 to exclude type-(c) errors (see below), and low power where the
noise is largest.
% to exclude surface-induced phase instability.
Errors from noise become small when multiple points are
averaged \citep[Ch.~1]{johnson1993ArraySignalProcessing}.

\textbf{(c)} Range-ambiguity errors result from multiple coherently
interfering echoes, a common case close to summits or in valleys
\citep{haacker2023SystematicErrorsObserved}. Affected waveform
samples have a reduced coherence, such that the largest errors are
discarded by the filter. However, the errors are still in the order of
10~\unit{m}, systematic, and affect consecutive waveform samples
similarly. \citet{haacker2023SystematicErrorsObserved} concluded that
this error can be reduced by averaging all available data from 89~days
and that comparing data separated by one or multiple years reduces the
error.
In the current work, we aggregate elevation estimates in 3-month rolling
windows, matching the 89~days approximately.
We do not restrict ourselves to only comparing yearly elevation
estimates because the overall uncertainty for the aggregated regions are
sufficiently small.
Restricting the data to yearly differences would come at the cost of
losing insights into seasonal variations.

Commonly, location errors show as elevation errors in the data. This is
important to be aware of when interpreting this data product. However,
the imposed coherence threshold and the consecutively applied maximum
elevation difference filter mitigate the impact of the errors on the
data product.


\subsection{Scattering horizon} \label{sec:errors:scat_horiz}

The transmitted Ku-band radar signal is reflected by matter depending on
its electromagnetic properties. Water, as well as firn, ice, and snow
(to some extent) reflect Ku-band radar. In general, two phenomena that
play a role in reflecting the radar signal are distinguished, namely,
surface scattering and volume scattering. ``Surface scattering''
describes the scattering at the surface due to the abrupt change in the
refractive index at the air-ice/snow interface. The part of the radar
wave that penetrates the surface is subject to ``volume scattering'',
i.e., it is scattered within the firn/snow volume by, e.g., internal
layers, grain-size contrasts, or ice lenses. Dry
snow reflects little of the Ku-band radar signal and, usually, can be
thought of as being
transparent, while layers of wet and dense snow are not penetrated if they
are thick enough.

With \cs, we can only observe the average echo for each waveform sample.
This results in a scattering horizon that, usually, is between the snow-air
interface and the glacial ice \citep{gray2019MeasuringHeightChange}.
The variable scattering horizon introduces a temporally correlated bias
that can also be spatially correlated and elevation dependent.
While often regarded as a source of uncertainty when considering
elevation changes, it was hypothesized to provide novel insights: e.g.,
\citet{morris2020SpreadSvalbardGlacier,
morris2022CryoSat2InterferometricMode} suggests the scattering
horizon change could be used to estimate the winter snow cover
thickness.

In summary, the observed surface elevation does not represent the snow-air
interface and the signal's penetration depth depends on the climatic
history. Thus, measurements of the same season are most comparable
because the mass above the scattering horizon is least different.
Similar to acknowledging the systematic location errors, type-(c), users
should be especially cautious when interpreting absolute elevation
estimates, or relative differences on time scales shorter than one year.


\subsection{Elevation uncertainty} \label{sec:errors:elevation}

The
complex relation between point elevation estimates and the rasterized
monthly elevation estimates does not permit using standard procedures to
derive the uncertainties. The major obstacle is the multimodal
distribution of elevation estimates within grid cells. We estimate the
uncertainty from the data spread given by the interquartile range scaled
to the equivalent of two standard deviations.
The range-ambiguity errors are already included, if points of multiple
tracks are aggregated, as with the 3-monthly aggregation period of the
current study.

When grid cells are spatially averaged, we consider a spatial
correlation distance of 2~\unit{km} \citep{jakob2023GlacierMassLoss} for
observed grid cells to compute the uncertainty $u_\text{obs}$.
Specifically, we sum the grid cell elevation uncertainties $u_{i,j}$ of
2 by 2~\unit{km} patches arithmetically, then compute the root sum of
squares across all patches, and divide through the number of observed
grid cells $N$ (Eq.~\ref{eq:errors:observed_cells}). 

\begin{equation} \label{eq:errors:observed_cells}
    u_\text{obs} = \frac{1}{N} \sqrt{
        \sum_\text{patches}\left(
            \sum_{i=1}^4\sum_{j=1}^4 u_{i,j}
        \right)^2
    }
\end{equation}

Errors in interpolated grid cells are correlated and biased with regard
to observed grid cells. We estimate bias and correlation based on
ArcticDEM strips. For each first-order region, we sample 10 randomly
chosen points and, again randomly, select 10 ArcticDEM strips covering
the sample point at different epochs, giving 100 strips in total, except for Alaska and
Scandinavia where only about 90 strips were available for each region.
We filter potentially compromised strip data excluding data where the
provided data quality flag is not zero. Further, we downsample the
2~\unit{m} resolution strip data using a two-step approach. Before
storing the data, we average to 32~\unit{m}, which greatly reduces
storage requirements. Then, we average to our 500~\unit{m} grid spacing.
In this reference data, we observed large differences to the companion
ArcticDEM mosaic product. We exclude these features by filtering the
strip data based on rolling 5 by 5 cell patches. We exclude patches
where either one value is more than 150~\unit{m} different from the
mosaic or where the minimum and maximum difference from the mosaic are
further apart than 150~\unit{m}.
For each ArcticDEM strip we compare the strip elevations with the
\cs-based elevation product at the time step closest to the strip
acquisition date. The \cs{} product contains both directly observed
elevations and elevations that were filled using one of the
interpolation methods. For every strip we therefore compute the mean
elevation difference between the \cs{} product and the ArcticDEM strip
separately for (i) grid cells based on direct \cs{} observations and
(ii) grid cells whose values were obtained using each interpolation
method. This yields one bias estimate per strip for the observed data
($b_{\text{ref}|0}$) and for each interpolation method ($b_{\text{ref}|1..6}$).
The differences $b_{0|1..6} = b_{\text{ref}|1..6} - b_{\text{ref}|0}$
then estimate the bias of each interpolation method relative to the
observed data. Aggregated by region, these differences are shown in the
appendix Fig.~\ref{fig:app:bias_by_interp}.
However, regional differences are small, and we do not treat
regions differently going forward. We derive the uncertainties of
interpolated data relative to observed data from the interquartile
range scaled to the equivalent of two standard deviations
$2\sigma_{0|1..6}$. Further, we calculate their correlations
$R_{i,j}$ across all strips. The uncertainty of spatially aggregated
elevation estimates $u_\text{sp.agg.}$ is then given by
Eq.~\ref{eq:unc:spatial_agg}, where $u_\text{obs}$ is based on
point-measurement interquartile ranges scaled to two standard deviations
(see above), $A_i$ is the area that was
interpolated with method $i$, and $A$ is the total aggregated area.

\begin{equation} \label{eq:unc:spatial_agg}
    u_\text{sp.agg.} = \frac{1}{A} \sqrt{
        \left(u_\text{obs} A_\text{obs}\right)^2 + \sum_{i=1}^6 { \sum_{j=1}^6 { \left(
            R_{i,j} \cdot 2\sigma_{0|i} A_i \cdot 2\sigma_{0|j} A_j
        \right) } }
    }
\end{equation}

Further, the errors are temporally correlated, which we consider in the
uncertainties of inter-year mass change estimates below. For each of the
sampled points, for each strip pair combination, we compute the
correlation of the bias, i.e., the differences of the current product to
the strips. The median correlation of biases across all pairs separated
by one year is $R_\text{1yr} = \VAR{coll.corr_1yr|fmt(prec=2,
in_math=True)}$ (see appendix Fig.~\ref{fig:app:one_yr_corr}). The
uncertainty $u_{t0,t1}$ of mass change estimates between year $t0$ and
$t1$ is then given by Eq.~\ref{eq:unc:temp_corr}.

\begin{equation} \label{eq:unc:temp_corr}
    u_{t0,t1} = \sqrt{u_{t0}^2 + u_{t1}^2 - 2 R_\text{1yr} u_{t0} u_{t1}}
\end{equation}


\subsection{Uncertainty from glacier outlines}
\label{sec:errors:coverage}

The glacier outlines from the RGI target the glaciers' extents at
the year 2000 but can be a few years earlier or later \citep[standard
deviation between 2 and 3 years]{rgi7}. However, our observational
period covers 2010 until 2024. We implemented an approach to detect
glacier-free area where there are no seasonal elevation changes at the
glacier front (Sect.~\ref{sec:algo:voids}, Step~2).
We treat the area identified as ice-free because of glacier retreat as
fully uncertain.

Moreover, four subregions were omitted completely due to insufficient
data coverage. These are
\VAR{coll.omitted_names}, accounting for
\VAR{coll.omitted_perc}~\unit{\%} of their parent regions, respectively.
For results for RGI first-order regions, we extrapolate the specific mass
balance of the observed area to these subregions that are entirely missing
from our trend product, using their glacier area from the RGI.
We assume this extrapolation to be fully uncertain, i.e., the contribution
from the extrapolated area is allowed to range from zero to twice the
extrapolated value.

We combine omitted subregions and retreat-related ice-free areas in a
single catch-all area uncertainty term, $u_{A1}$, because both contribute
to the area mismatch between our trend product and the RGI.
Specifically, $u_{A1}$ is defined as the area difference between the
glacier area covered by our trend product and the glacier area according
to the RGI (Table~\ref{tab:subreg_overview}).
When subregions are aggregated, we assume these
errors are fully correlated and sum them arithmetically. Expressed
relative to the glacierized area of each RGI first-order region,
$u_{A1}$ ranges between \VAR{coll.area_diff.min|fmt(prec=1)} for
\VAR{coll.area_diff.min.name} and
\VAR{coll.area_diff.max|fmt(prec=1)} for
\VAR{coll.area_diff.max.name}.


Additionally, the outlines of glaciers from the RGI are inherently
imperfect. For the outdated RGI version 3.2, the estimated uncertainties of
the glacierized areas in regions of the Arctic except Scandinavia range
from \VAR{coll.rgi_unc.Iceland|fmt(prec=1)} for Iceland to
\VAR{coll.rgi_unc["Greenland Periphery"]|fmt(prec=1)} for Greenland
Periphery \citep[updated for RGI v7.0
outlines]{pfeffer2014RandolphGlacierInventory}. Scandinavia's glacier
areas are uncertain to \VAR{coll.rgi_unc.Scandinavia|fmt(prec=1)}. In absence of a systematic
uncertainty evaluation of the glacier areas in the current RGI version
7.0, we use the older uncertainty estimation, specifically, the
area-dependent Eq.~\ref{eq:err:rgi_area} with (individual) glacier area $s_i$ and
parameters $k=3$, $e_1=0.039$, and $p=0.70$
\citep[Eq.~1]{pfeffer2014RandolphGlacierInventory}. This uncertainty
estimate is assumed to be regionally (RGI first-order regions)
correlated \citep{pfeffer2014RandolphGlacierInventory}, and is summed
arithmetically resulting in $u_{A2}$ with $i$ in ${1, .., N}$ for $N$
glaciers in the concerned region (Eq.~\ref{eq:err:rgi_sum},
Table~\ref{tab:subreg_overview}). The total area uncertainty $u_A$ is
calculated in Eq.~\ref{eq:err:area}.
\begin{gather}
    e(s_i) = k e_1 s_i^p := 3 \cdot 0.039 \cdot s_i^{0.70} \label{eq:err:rgi_area} \\
    u_{A2}(s_1,..,s_N) = \sum_i e(s_i) \label{eq:err:rgi_sum} \\
    u_A = \sqrt{u_{A1}^2 + u_{A2}^2} \label{eq:err:area}
\end{gather}


\subsection{Uncertainty propagation to higher-level results}

We estimate the uncertainties of multi-year average surface elevation
trends $u_{\dot{h}}$ by two standard deviations of the fitted model
parameter (Sect.~\ref{sec:algo:regional_trend}). Assuming a density
uncertainty of glacial ice volume changes for long time scales
$u_{\rho,\text{long}} = 60$~\unit{kg}~\unit{m^{-3}}
\citep{huss2013DensityAssumptionsConverting}, the mass balance ($\dot{M}$)
uncertainty $u_{\dot{M}}$ follows in Eq.~\ref{eq:errors:mass_balance}
from uncertainty propagation, with the surface elevation trend $\dot{h}$
and other symbols like above.

\begin{equation} \label{eq:errors:mass_balance}
    u_{\dot{M}} = \sqrt{
        (A\rho \cdot u_{\dot{h}})^2
        + (A\dot{h} \cdot u_{\rho,\text{long}})^2
        + (\rho\dot{h} \cdot u_A)^2
    }
\end{equation}

Further, we compute mass changes in single hydrological years which we
define to start in September. For single years, we assume a larger
density uncertainty of $u_{\rho,\text{short}} =
150$~\unit{kg}~\unit{m^{-3}}
\citep{huss2013DensityAssumptionsConverting}. For the mass change
from year $t0$ to year $t1$ with elevation change $\Delta h_{t0,t1}$, Equation~\ref{eq:errors:mass_change}
follows from propagating the uncertainties.
\begin{equation} \label{eq:errors:mass_change}
    u_{\Delta M} = \sqrt{
        (A\rho \cdot u_{t0,t1})^2
        + (A\,{\Delta h_{t0,t1}} \cdot u_{\rho,\text{short}})^2
        + (\rho\,{\Delta h_{t0,t1}} \cdot u_A)^2
    }
\end{equation}
For single-year mass changes, the elevation change uncertainty
is about 20 to 100 times larger than the other contributions.


\section{Results} \label{sec:results}

The trend and monthly data products are published in georeferenced
TIFF and netCDF formats, respectively, with each subregion in a separate
file.
The data are projected onto coordinate systems listed in Table~\ref{tab:subreg_overview} at a spatial
resolution of 500 by 500~\unit{m}. 
The trend product contains the trend data according to Sect.~\ref{sec:algo:elev_trend}
and its standard deviation.
The monthly product's main variable is the surface elevation difference from ArcticDEM.
Additionally, we provide the standard deviation and the number of point
elevation estimates that the aggregates are based on. Note that, while we consistently use
$2\sigma$-uncertainties in the current manuscript, the dataset follows
NetCDF Climate and Forecast (CF) Metadata Conventions version 1.12
\citep{eaton2024NetCDFClimateForecast} and includes a single standard
deviation, as also specified in the metadata.
Further, the dataset includes the reference elevation that the elevation
differences refer to, the number of aggregated point measurements, and
a flag that indicates the applied interpolation strategy.
See the data availability section and the corresponding data description
at the repository for the metadata in full detail.

For comparison, the EOLIS monthly gridded product described for Baseline 3
uses a 2~\unit{km} grid and a three-month rolling window, and includes
elevation uncertainty. Its point product combines swath and POCA
observations, identified by an \verb|is_swath| variable
\citep{cryotempo-eolis2026CryoTEMPOEOLISProductHandbook}, whereas our
dataset is swath-only. Finer grid spacing
does not by itself establish greater observational accuracy.

