Global glacier elevation and mass changes were derived earlier using
\cs{} swath data from August 2010 to August 2020
\citep{jakob2023GlacierMassLoss}. In all regions, our results for the
period from August 2010 until August 2020 agree well with the earlier
results (Table~\ref{tab:jg23_comparison}). By contrast, the results of a
dataset from photogrammetry at 100~\unit{m} resolution
\citep{hugonnet2021AcceleratedGlobalGlacier} differ from the current
study for a number of regions (Table~\ref{tab:regional_mass_balances}).
The relative difference is largest for Iceland where they find a $-7.7
\pm 0.6$~\unit{Gt}~\unit{y^{-1}} mass-balance rate from January 2010 until
December 2019, while we only find \VAR{trends[("Iceland", "10_19")]|r2v("mass")|fmt(prec=1)}
for a similar period from August 2010 until December 2019.
The good agreement with the earlier \cs-based study of
\citet{jakob2023GlacierMassLoss} hints at sensor differences as the central cause
for the discrepancy with the photogrammetry dataset of
\citet{hugonnet2021AcceleratedGlobalGlacier}.
We will, however, take a closer look because a constant bias between the
compared methods could not explain the different change rates.
Both datasets, the \cs- and the photogrammetry-based, use the same
volume change density assumption; hence, we can rule this out as a cause
for the observed difference.
