% \subsection{Coverage}

The coverage, which we define as the proportion of observed
trends (as opposed to interpolated trends) to the total number of
glacierized grid cells, varies much between regions.
The coverage of Alaska Range (Wrangell/Kilbuck) is the lowest with
\VAR{cover_df.at[("Alaska", "Alaska_Range-Wrangell-Kilbuck"),
"coverage"]|P(1)}; the coverage of Melville Island, Arctic Canada North,
is the highest with \VAR{cover_df.at[("Arctic_Canada_North",
"Melville_Island"), "coverage"]|P(1)}.
Next to the operational mode (SARIn or other) and the meridional track
convergence, the fraction of the glacier surface inside the considered
footprint that is illuminated by \cs's signal determines the coverage.
Simplifying, if the range of elevations within the footprint is less
than 240~\unit{m}, in an ideal case, \cs{} could sample the entire
surface.
However, if the elevation range is larger, this is no longer possible.
\begin{figure}[t]
    \includegraphics[width=8.3cm]{\VAR{"fig16__coverage_terraincomplexity_correlation.pdf"|fig}}
    \caption{Percentage of directly observed grid cells with regard to
        a terrain complexity index, the footprint topography 10-90-span
        (see main text). Each point shows the
        aggregates for one subregion and the line shows a linear model
        fit. The coverage decays with increasing 10-90-span.}
    \label{fig:disc:coverage_vs_steepness}
\end{figure}
To visualize the actual relation between coverage and the elevation
range in Fig.~\ref{fig:disc:coverage_vs_steepness}, we define a terrain
complexity index that we refer to as footprint topography 10-90-span.
This is the elevation difference between the tenth percentile and the
90th percentile of the surface hypsometry within each 15 by 15~\unit{km}
square that includes glaciers.
Least-squares fitting a linear model to the data results in
\VAR{cover_fit.trend|fmt} coverage per 100~\unit{m} of 10-90-span ($p
\ll 0.05$).
The lack of coverage is, at least roughly, proportional to the terrain
complexity index. Together with the offset of \VAR{cover_fit.const|fmt}
coverage for a hypothetical zero inter-quantile range (10-90-span), this
insight could be used to estimate the expected \cs{} SARIn coverage
for other regions. However, extrapolating this estimate to other regions
should be limited to comparable latitudes, i.e.,
poleward of about $\pm60\unit{^\circ}$, and the here covered terrain
complexities.


% \subsection{Hypsometric interpolation}
% \label{sec:disc:interp}

Earlier, we concluded that a part of the disagreement with reference
data (\VAR{vatna_df.ratio.interp|P(0)} in the case of Vatnajökull) can
be attributed to a biased interpolation. We interpolate voids using the
state-of-the-art local mean hypsometric interpolation that has been
found to perform best by \citet{mcnabb2019SensitivityGlacierVolume}.
Our implementation adds weighting and ridge regularization
(Sect.~\ref{sec:algo:voids}) to this approach. The underlying
method bins elevation change data corresponding to bands of elevation in
which the changes occur, fits a third-order polynomial to the changes,
and integrates the fitted model folded with the hypsometry. Despite
being the best established method, it has not been evaluated
specifically for radar altimetry data. We find that no data being
available for the highest or lowest elevation bands complicates this
method because the fitted polynomial needs to be extrapolated. Other
established methods, e.g., kriging methods
\citep{strossenreuther2020HowDifferentAnalysis}, do not consider the
elevation dependence. However, the elevation dependence could be
included for kriging on mountain glaciers. The community would profit
from dedicated research to study how voids add to the overall mass
change uncertainty derived from \cs{} data for, specifically, mountain
glaciers.


% \subsection{A swath-only product}

The presented dataset is a swath-only dataset.
We explicitly refrain from merging the additionally derived POCA data or
from swath-processing the leading edge.
There is no straightforward method to combine POCA and swath data.
While it may seem odd to discard information, using them requires
research on how the elevation estimates of the two datasets relate to
each other. Note that the bias between POCA and swath data depends on
the topography and the surface properties and will vary spatially and
temporally. Further note, that POCA data should likely be weighted more
than swath data because POCA data are based on multiple waveform
samples.
Successfully integrating POCA data could yield benefits around
mountain tops and ice divides, especially.
Elevation estimates from swath processing the leading edge are similarly
biased with regard to the remainder of the waveform
\citep{haacker2023SystematicErrorsObserved}. The associated received
signal is reflected above the swath scattering horizon.


% \subsection{Surging glaciers}

The product shows the expected fingerprint of surging
glaciers in, e.g., Svalbard: gains at low elevations and losses at high
elevations (Appendix~\ref{app:svalbard_surges},
Fig.~\ref{fig:app:svalbard_surges}).
Thus, CryoSwath is a promising tool to search for interesting study
areas.
The hypsometric change rate profiles could be used to automate finding
potentially surging glaciers.
With the versatile API, these profiles could easily be calculated using
the \lstinline|cryoswath.misc.interpolate_hypsometrically()| function and used
for comparison on, e.g., a yearly basis.


