\conclusions  %% \conclusions[modified heading if necessary]

In this work, we present the Python package CryoSwath 0.2.2, which
allows deriving surface elevation data from ESA's \cs{} SARIn L1b
product at Baseline E.
The workflow supports automated data filtering, resolving the phase ambiguity, locating the
scatterer distributions, rasterizing the point elevation estimates, and
interpolating any voids to arrive at monthly sampled, three-month rolling-window estimates on a 500 by
500~\unit{m} grid. At the same time, users have a number of options to
customize the processing within each step.
CryoSwath provides means for researchers without prior SARIn swath
processing knowledge to derive elevation change estimates for glacier
surfaces. Being open-source, CryoSwath provides transparency and the
ability to modify its implementation. While CryoSwath offers pipelines
to immediately compute
high-level products, the intermediate results can be inspected at any
time. CryoSwath
optionally retains waveform diagnostics such as power and coherence
in its point elevation outputs. Its modularity allows adding
diagnostic output beyond the implemented options and, generally,
replacing parts of the workflow according to custom demands.
As MIT-licensed open-source software, CryoSwath can be adopted
and advanced by the community.
% We summarize causes that can lead to location errors, describe the
% phenomenon of changing scattering horizons, and provide an adequate
% method to estimate uncertainties.

The presented surface elevation data covers several first-order RGI
regions: Alaska, Arctic Canada North and South, the Greenland Periphery,
Iceland, Svalbard and Jan Mayen, Scandinavia, and the Russian
Arctic. This dataset enables detailed analysis of the evolution and
dynamics of individual glaciers, opening new avenues for follow-up
research.
Among the regions studied, glaciers in Alaska experienced the greatest
ice loss, both in absolute and relative terms, with a mass loss of
\VAR{trends[("Alaska", "___")]|r2v("mass")|fmt(absolute=True)} and a
surface lowering of \VAR{trends[("Alaska",
"___")]|r2v("spec")|fmt(prec=2, absolute=True)}. Across all regions and
subregions, surface elevation is decreasing. The smallest rates of
surface lowering are observed in Arctic Canada North and the Greenland
Periphery, with \VAR{trends[("Arctic Canada North",
"___")]|r2v("spec")|fmt(style="nounits", prec=2, absolute=True)} and
\VAR{trends[("Greenland Periphery",
"___")]|r2v("spec")|fmt(absolute=True)}, respectively.
Within Iceland, the surface elevation of its ice caps
shows localized increases, particularly over Vatnajökull, the country's
largest ice cap. These elevation gains occur mainly at higher altitudes,
while lower elevations continue to show significant losses. However,
photogrammetric observations do not fully agree in the magnitude of
these patterns, suggesting a possible discrepancy between changes in
scattering-horizon elevation derived from \cs{} data and changes in the
actual surface elevation observed by photogrammetry.
Investigating differences in elevation change rates between
photogrammetry and radar altimetry may offer insights into firn
densification processes \citep{otosaka2020SurfaceMeltingDrives}.
Moreover, the sign of the discrepancy between the two measurement
techniques differs across regions. In Arctic Canada North and South, the
Greenland Periphery, and Iceland, the photogrammetry-based mass balances
are more negative, while in Svalbard and the Russian Arctic, \cs-derived
trends are more negative. An
Arctic-wide comparison of both methods could thus provide clues about
regional climatological shifts.


