\subsection{Echo-coordinate derivation}
\label{app:geolocation}

The variables and reference frame are defined in
Sect.~\ref{sec:algo:geolocation} and illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:sketch}.

Below, we will calculate the echo origin location using the satellite
position, its travel direction, the great-circle distance to the echo
origin, and its elevation above the reference ellipsoid. The last two of
the four are not yet known. With Eq.~\ref{eq:Pscat}, we start off by
calculating the length of $\vec{P_\text{scat}}$. We then compute the
angle $\Delta \zeta$ between $\vec{P_\text{CS2}}$ and
$\vec{P_\text{scat}}$ in Eq.~\ref{eq:delZeta}.
We continue in Eq.~\ref{eq:curvature} \citep[Ch.~4,
Eq.~4.15]{torge2012Geodesy} by calculating the Earth's radius of
curvature $r$ at nadir in the prime vertical plane. This is a good
approximation because at the poles where the angle between \cs's
across-track direction and the prime vertical plane is largest, the
meridional radius of curvature approaches the radius of curvature in the
prime vertical plane.
Here, $a$ and $e$ refer to the WGS84 semi-major axis length and the
eccentricity, respectively.
Next, we calculate the elevation of the echo origin $h$ in
Eq.~\ref{eq:elevation}.
Lastly, in Eq.~\ref{eq:offNadirD}, we approximate the ellipsoidal
distance between nadir and the echo origin by the great-circle distance
$d_\text{off}$ with the curvature at nadir.

\begin{gather}
    \begin{split}
        |\vec{P_\text{scat}}| & = \sqrt{(\vec{P_\text{CS2}}-(\vec{P_\text{CS2}}-\vec{P_\text{scat}}))^2} \\
            & = \sqrt{|\vec{P_\text{CS2}}|^2
          - 2\cdot \cos(\theta) \, |\vec{P_\text{CS2}}| \, \overline{P_\text{CS2}P_\text{scat}}
            + \overline{P_\text{CS2}P_\text{scat}}^2} \label{eq:Pscat}
    \end{split} \\
    \Delta \zeta = \arctan\left(
        \frac{|\vec{P_\text{scat}}| \, \sin(\theta)}{|\vec{P_\text{CS2}}| - |\vec{P_\text{scat}}| \, \cos(\theta)}
        \right) \label{eq:delZeta} \\
    r = \frac{a}{\sqrt{1-e^2\sin^2(\phi)}} \label{eq:curvature} \\
    h = |\vec{P_\text{scat}}| - r \label{eq:elevation} \\
    d_\text{off} = r \cdot \Delta \zeta \label{eq:offNadirD}
\end{gather}

Now, having \cs's coordinates $\phi$ and $\lambda$, the direction to the
echo origin $\alpha + 90\unit{^\circ}$, and the great-circle distance
$d_\text{off}$ between nadir and the echo origin, we use \texttt{pyproj}
\citep{snow2025Pyproj4Pyproj371} to compute the echo-origin
coordinates.

\subsection{Svalbard surge examples}
\label{app:svalbard_surges}

Surface elevation trends for Svalbard are shown in
Fig.~\ref{fig:app:svalbard_surges}.
Multiple glaciers on Svalbard show thinning at higher elevations and
thickening at the glacier fronts, indicating surge phases that, indeed,
were reported earlier.
Those are Wahlenbergbreen \citep{sevestre2018TidewaterGlacierSurges},
Penckbreen and Sveitsarfonna
\citep{mccerery2024TerrestrialGlacialGeomorphology},
Morsjnevbreen/Strongbreen \citep{kaab2023GlobalClusteringRecent},
and Recherchebreen \citep{kavan2024GlacierSurgeTrigger}.
Arnesenbreen \citep{kaab2023GlobalClusteringRecent} shows the same
signature, but less evident.
On Austfonna, the most pronounced surge patterns are seen on
Storisstraumen (also known as Basin-3) and the neighboring Basin-2
\citep{dunse2015GlaciersurgeMechanismsPromoted,
morris2022CryoSat2InterferometricMode}.

